From 4f2dfb77b569f919400a24bd791161532ac7d4ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: FrontierAIAccountabilityProject
 <315748336+FrontierAIAccountabilityProject@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 12:22:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] house language: never write the bare 'deployer' -- the
 exclusion the text does not give

The 'Not about' boilerplate listed DEPLOYERS among the classes the Act does
not reach, in nine files. That was already imprecise against the tagged text
and is now contradicted by the project's own front page.

The tagged statute has never excluded deployers. SEC. 1(b)(3) defines
deployer as a covered class; SEC. 2(b) gives a non-modifying deployer a way
to DISCHARGE the duty -- adopt the upstream validation, keep the manifest,
monitor, report -- which is a conditional reliance rule, not an exemption
(and E5 already logged the missing de-minimis rule for thin deployers). On
top of that, CURE 7 reaches deployer-integrators at consequential scale, the
front-page coverage table names two of them, and known_objections answers
the platform objection with 'covered as deployers of what they deploy'. A
hostile reader had a free shot: your own page says you don't reach
deployers, and your own table names deployment companies.

Rewritten in nine files rather than patched in one:
- the long form (house_language, commentary_sweep, for_legislators,
  already_a_crime_for_you, docs/questions, docs/the_case) now reads
  'ordinary deployers and API customers' and carries the precise position --
  always a covered class, always a discharge route for the thin ones, CURE 7
  only at consequential scale and only for a deployer's own deployment
  decisions, never as developer of a model it did not train.
- the short variants (what_these_words_mean, why_the_disparity,
  why_a_signature_works) and the census variant point to the full statement
  at docs/the_case.md#who-this-is-about, which gains that anchor.
- house_language gains the drafting rule itself: write 'ordinary deployer',
  'API customer', or 'deployer operating within a validated configuration',
  never the bare 'deployer'. The reassurance stays true by being specific --
  duties climb toward the people who decide what a frontier system is and
  where it acts; they do not land on a company that bought access to one.

All relative links verified. Tagged v3.4 text unchanged (E10).
